Found while verifying karr #87 (Ollama Cloud auth) against a real server.

Langertha::Engine::Ollama::chat_response passes Ollama's `created_at` straight into
Langertha::Response:

  lib/Langertha/Engine/Ollama.pm:313
    $data->{created_at} ? ( created => $data->{created_at} ) : (),

But Response.created is typed `Maybe[Int]` (lib/Langertha/Response.pm:250-254, "Unix
timestamp"), while the native Ollama API returns an RFC3339 string
("2026-08-17T02:58:35.885433013Z"). Every non-streaming chat against a real Ollama server
therefore dies in the Response constructor:

  Attribute (created) does not pass the type constraint because:
  Validation failed for 'Maybe[Int]' with value 2026-02-22T04:00:45.027209927Z

Reproducible offline with the repo's own captured fixture, no network:

  perl -Ilib -MHTTP::Response -MLangertha::Engine::Ollama -e '
    my $body = do { local $/; open my $fh, "<", "t/data/ollama_tool_call_response.json"; <$fh> };
    my $o = Langertha::Engine::Ollama->new(url=>"http://x.invalid:11434", model=>"m");
    my $http = HTTP::Response->new(200,"OK",["Content-Type"=>"application/json"],$body);
    eval { $o->chat_response($http) }; print $@;
  '

Also hit live against https://ollama.com with gpt-oss:20b (2026-08-17) — the HTTP call and
the auth succeed, the response object construction is what dies.

Why the suite is green: t/64_tool_calling_ollama_mock.t drives chat_with_tools_f, and
Langertha::Role::Tools:424 works on the raw parse_response HashRef — it never builds a
Langertha::Response. The croaking path is simple_chat / chat_f / chat_response, which only
the live tests (t/83_live_chat.t, gated on TEST_LANGERTHA_OLLAMA_URL) exercise.

Pre-existing since 1fac6c4 (2026-02-22).

Fix shape (needs a decision, hence a ticket, not a drive-by): either parse the RFC3339
stamp to epoch seconds in chat_response, or widen Response.created. The engine-agnostic
seconds-vs-native-stages precedent from ADR 0011 (timing) suggests the engine converts and
Response stays a Unix timestamp. A unit test on the fixture would have caught it and should
come with the fix.
Fixed in the engine (not in Response). Engine::Ollama::_created_at_to_epoch converts the
RFC3339 created_at to epoch seconds before it reaches Response.created; Maybe[Int] stays
honest and the native string stays under raw.created_at -- the same normalized-plus-native
split the *_seconds timing keys already use (ADR 0011). Unparseable stamps (incl. Ollama's
"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z" sentinel) drop the field instead of croaking. Epoch-shaped values from
shims pass through numified, so TO_JSON keeps emitting a number.

Widening Response.created was rejected: created has exactly two producers
(Role::OpenAICompatible with an epoch Int, and Ollama), so a coercion at the value object
would buy nothing and would either loosen the type for all 7 Response->new call sites or need
a subtype nobody else uses.

Regression gate in t/70_response.t: two subtests, +2 top-level tests (74 -> 76). One drives
chat_response over the captured server fixtures t/data/ollama_tool_call_response.json and
ollama_tool_result_response.json; the other covers the created_at normalization matrix
(fractional/whole-second UTC, +/- offsets with and without colon, epoch int and epoch string,
unparseable/sentinel/empty -> field dropped and response still built) and asserts TO_JSON
still serializes created as a JSON number. Both subtests fail against HEAD without the
engine fix.

Verified offline, no live calls: repro green, prove -lr t/ green, perlcritic clean.
Working tree only, not committed: lib/Langertha/Engine/Ollama.pm, lib/Langertha/Response.pm
(POD of created only), t/70_response.t.

